# 9. Data analysis plan

| Notebook | Question | Required charts / outputs |
|---|---|---|
| 01_ka_quality | Which feeds/places have usable coverage? | Coverage heatmap by source/day; null fraction bars; duplicate rate timeline; reporting-lag ECDF; source-status table with denominators. |
| 02_ka_rain | How do gauges differ across space/seasons? | Daily rain calendar; seasonal boxplots; gauge-vs-grid scatter and bias; variogram; Moran residual map with significance after correction. |
| 03_ka_prices | Are reported spikes real and predictable? | Market commodity price lines; missing-day calendar; seasonal decomposition; rain/price lag plots; class prevalence by market; unit/outlier audit. |
| 04_bmtc_coverage | Who lacks scheduled access? | Stops/route map;500m walking catchments; population-weighted coverage by ward; weekday trips/hour histogram; low-supply vs population scatter. No assertion of service reliability. |
| 05_kl_flood2018 | Where and when did documented impacts occur? | Dated event timeline; rainfall accumulation map; dam-level panel with missingness; district impacts; source-resolution comparison; reconstructed vs as-known legend. |
| 06_rain_price | How do prices move after extreme rain? | Event-study lead/lag coefficients with95%CI; pre-trend joint-test result; matched sample balance; placebo distribution; commodity heterogeneity with FDR labels. |
| 07_climate | How has rainfall changed and what do projections show? | 1901-2024 district anomalies; Theil-Sen slope+CI map; Mann-Kendall FDR map;1981-2010 vs2021-2050 seasonal distributions by CMIP6 model; monsoon cumulative anomaly. |
| 08_delhi_transit | Which routes suffer unreliable headways? | Arrival delay ECDF; time-of-day MAE; route headway distributions; bunching timeline; missing-GPS map; matched-label retention funnel. |
| 09_delhi_air | Do station corrections beat raw CAMS? | Station forecast overlays and interval bands; horizon MAE/CRPS; observed-vs-predicted scatter; residual season/hour plots; route ambient exposure map. |
| models/m01..m20 | Why did a candidate pass or fail? | Target histogram/prevalence; baseline/candidate paired errors; residuals or PR curve; interval reliability or calibration curve; spatial/time slices; SHAP/feature permutation; failure cases; learning curve and run-cost table. |

Every figure includes source IDs, date span, sample count, geography/vintage, units and missing-data handling. Save PNG/SVG plus chart data CSV/Parquet. Notebook logic calls tested package functions; no manual spreadsheet fixes. Execute from clean kernel using pinned manifests and fixed seed42.

### Exact dashboard metrics

| Dashboard | Metrics and denominator |
|---|---|
| Overview / Place Lens | Latest observed/valid/issued time; freshness seconds; active official alerts count after expiry/cancellation; valid records/expected records; model status; price in INR/quintal with market and date. |
| Data quality | missing_fraction=missing_expected/expected; duplicate_fraction=duplicate_raw/raw; invalid_geometry_count; out_of_range_count; schema_failure_count; fetch_successes/attempts; p50/p95 reporting lag; last_success; archive_bytes/cap. |
| Model accuracy | MAE=sum(abs(error))/n, RMSE=sqrt(mean(errorÂ²)), PR-AUC with prevalence, Brier mean((p-y)Â²), calibration bins10 equal-count,80% interval coverage, mean interval width, pinball/CRPS; paired baseline difference and95%CI. |
| Forecast monitoring | Matured predictions/issued predictions; unresolved-label age; last retrain; missing feature percentage; PSI;14-day labelled MAE ratio; approval status and rollback version. |
| Transit coverage | Population within500m routable walk/total covered population; scheduled trips per hour by service day; matched stop arrivals/observed passages; ETA MAE and p90 absolute error, headway coefficient of variation. |
| Climate and risk | Area-weighted rainfall anomaly mm and percent; reference period; population in union of risk zones/covered population; NDVI valid-pixel fraction; projection model spread. No casualty estimates. |
| Comparison | Same date range, spatial aggregation and units only; show coverage ratio side by side. Per-capita prices are nonsensical; normalize exposures by population and rainfall by climate reference separately. |

# 10. Data science plan

| Hypothesis | Design / assumptions | Decision rule / caution |
|---|---|---|
| H1 Rain extremes precede price spikes | Predefine exposure above training seasonal95th percentile; distributed lags0..4weeks; market-commodity and calendar-week fixed effects; market/week clustered SE. Avoid post-treatment arrivals as controls. | Report pre-trend joint test and sensitivity to1/2/4week windows. BH q<=0.05 across commodity/lag family. Association wording unless parallel trends, timing and confounders defended. |
| H2 Spatial rain interpolation helps held-out gauges | Leave station spatial blocks out; contiguous outage masks; compare kriging, inverse-distance and ML on identical genuine observations. Fit variogram only on training geometry/data. | Paired district/storm block bootstrap1000; improvement lower95%CI>0. Include altitude/distance strata; explain missing-not-at-random outage bias. |
| H3 Flood residuals cluster spatially | Queen-contiguity admin weights or H3 neighbour graph, row standardised; Moran I with999 spatial permutations for each time block. Inspect residuals rather than only raw outcomes. | BH correction across district/time tests. Significant clustering suggests model misspecification; not evidence of contagion or causality. |
| H4 Historical rain trend differs across districts | Area-weighted IMD series; Theil-Sen slope; block-bootstrap CI; modified Mann-Kendall accounting for autocorrelation. Missing years remain gaps. | FDR across districts; compare period1901-2024 vs1951-2024; gauge-network and boundary changes acknowledged. No exact attribution to climate change from trend alone. |
| H5 CAMS residual model improves station forecasts | Same issue time, pollutant, location, averaging window and available data for baseline/candidate; paired station/day errors. | Lower95%CI of MAE improvement>0 and >=10% point improvement; no baseline built from future updated CAMS runs. |
| H6 Scheduled transit coverage is inequitable | Population-weighted500m network catchments and service frequency. Compare ward coverage distributions, not individual households. | Report bootstrap uncertainty for network/coverage sampling and scenario sensitivity400/500/800m; fixed map coverage itself is a census-of-data, not a sample needing arbitrary p-values. |
| H7 Karnataka model transfers to Kerala | Freeze KA training first; harmonize target/sampling/feature definitions; compare zero-shot, local logistic and recalibrated models on same held-out KL events. | Report negative transfer. Recalibration uses KL validation only; never use2018 test for selecting a2022-trained model. |
| H8 Anomaly rules separate data errors from events | Two labels: data-quality defect vs genuine extreme; review sample with source evidence; rule and IsolationForest share identical evaluation set. | Precision/alert burden with CI; no automatic deletion or hazard escalation from unsupervised anomaly score. |

Granger analysis: test stationarity using ADF/KPSS with explicit trend choice, difference price/rain series when justified, choose lag1..4weeks using training AIC, examine residual autocorrelation and apply HAC/cluster-aware inference. Passing a Granger test means past rain helps predict price conditional on included lags; it does not establish a causal effect.

Causal alternative: where timing and comparison markets are defensible, use a matched event study with not-yet-exposed controls, check overlap and pre-trends, and sensitivity to spillovers/transport closures. If weather shocks affect neighbours or selection into reporting, do not report a clean treatment effect. Instrumental-variable designs are rejected without an externally justified instrument.

Uncertainty units match dependence: time-series moving blocks length7days (30days for seasonal/long-horizon reservoir studies), entire storm/flood events for hazard labels, service days/routes for transit, catchment blocks for landslides. Run sensitivity block lengths7/14/30 where feasible. Small independent-event counts are reported directly; a narrow row-wise interval is not a substitute.

# 11. ML model cards

### Shared model-card contract: applies to every model below

Every run records model ID/version, intended user, prohibited use, exact target and units, label provenance, dataset hash, feature available-at rules, dated split manifest, sample/event counts, baseline, candidate, hyperparameters, metric confidence intervals, calibration, limitations and retraining decision. Random time-series splits are prohibited. A future data-availability test determines feasibility; no dataset or skill is assumed to exist.

| Protocol | Exact implementation |
|---|---|
| Leakage control | All rolling features end at issue time minus stated publication lag; if observed publication lag is longer, use max(stated,measured). Fit scalers, imputation, quantile thresholds, class weights, variograms and feature selection in training only. Purge overlapping labels by maximum forecast horizon. Spatial folds keep event/catchment groups together. |
| Weather vintages | Historical ERA5/reanalysis is not an archived weather forecast. If actual issue-time forecasts cannot be retrieved, train past-weather-only model and label retrospective scoring hindcast. Operational forecast-feature learner is calibrated only after enough real prospective vintages accumulate. |
| Protocol R: regression | Report MAE primary, RMSE, median absolute error and skill1-MAE_model/MAE_baseline. Avoid MAPE for zeros/near-zero and degreesC.80% quantiles q10/q50/q90, validation-only conformal residual adjustment; report coverage and width. CRPS from available ensembles, or clearly labelled approximate quantile CRPS. |
| Protocol C: classification | PR-AUC primary with prevalence; Brier/calibration and alert burden; ROC-AUC/F1 secondary. Train-only class_weight balanced capped10; never SMOTE across time. Choose decision threshold on tuning set; isotonic only with>=200 calibration positives, otherwise logistic/Platt or no calibration with warning. |
| Protocol A: anomaly/inference/scenario | M10 uses coefficient/clustered-SE inference; M16 precision and false alert rate; M20 deterministic sensitivity checks. Do not invent classification accuracy for a no-label anomaly system or confidence in an unobserved counterfactual. |
| Baseline fairness | Same rows, target units, covariates available at issue, geographic resolution and forecast horizon. Compare to strongest predeclared simple baseline, not whichever is easiest to beat. Fold score and held-out score separated. |
| Search budget | Optuna TPE seed42,30 trials maximum,2 CPU hours/model,2 parallel trials with2GB combined budget; early-stop each tree fit after50 non-improving rounds. Tune only validation, never test; neural search max3 manual configs under its GPU cap. |
| Tree search | LightGBM n_estimators100..1000, learning_rate{0.02,0.05,0.1}, num_leaves{7,15,31}, max_depth{3,5,8,-1}, min_child_samples{20,50,100}, feature_fraction{0.7,1},lambda_l2{0,1,10}; quantile alpha fixed0.1/0.5/0.9. |
| Other search | Ridge alpha{0.1,1,10,100}; logistic C{0.01,0.1,1,10}; RF trees{200,500}, depth{5,10,None}, min_leaf{5,20}; SARIMAX p,q0..2,d0..1, seasonal P/Q0..1 with stated7/365 period only if enough data; kriging spherical/exponential/gaussian variograms fit on train; IsolationForest100/300 trees, contamination{0.005,0.01,0.02}. |
| Promotion | Candidate must pass model-specific threshold AND lower95% paired bootstrap CI of primary improvement>0; calibration/coverage and critical-group checks pass.1000 resamples seed42. If intervals too wide or data insufficient, baseline stays champion. No automatic claim that a proposed algorithm will win. |
| Explainability | TreeSHAP on fixed<=200-row stratified training background; local top5 features plus direction and units. Linear coefficients for linear models; permutation importance on validation. GRU integrated-gradients optional only if validated; U-Net show mask/error overlays, not fake tabular SHAP. Explanations describe model behaviour, not causes. |
| Retraining/drift | Daily score matured forecasts; monthly candidate retraining for price/reservoir/AQI/ETA; drought quarterly; susceptibility/news/segmentation only after audited new labels. PSI>0.2 in2 consecutive windows or labelled MAE>1.2x baseline for14days triggers review, never blind promotion. For probabilities monitor Brier and event coverage; low label count delays decisions. |
| Reproducibility | Train seeds42/43/44 for finalist; report seed spread. Freeze train/tune/calibrate/test dates in registry; serialize feature order, transformations and library locks. Prefer LightGBM text/native formats, safetensors for neural weights; never unpickle untrusted models. |

### M01 - Bengaluru ward flood occurrence

| Card field | Specification |
|---|---|
| Scope | KA; v1; classification. Not an emergency decision system. |
| Target / label / horizon / unit | Ward x issue day; probability of >=1 independently documented inundation in the next 24 h. Negatives require a monitored ward-day with explicit coverage, not missing reports. Labels S43; horizon 24 h. |
| Features and lag evidence | S27 antecedent rain sums 1/3/7 days ending t-1; S01 forecast rain only from archived issue<=t; S14 slope/elevation; S18 impervious/road density at release<=t; S43 prior incidents lag>=7d; static drainage proximity if verified. |
| Train / tune / calibration / test and CV | Train 2017-01-01..2022-12-31; tune 2023-01-01..2023-06-30; calibrate remaining 2023; test 2024. Rolling monsoon-year origins; 5 spatial ward blocks with no shared event in training/test. |
| Baselines | Training ward-season prevalence; rain-threshold rule selected on validation. |
| Candidate ladder / provisional final choice | Logistic regression, random forest, then LightGBM classifier; provisional LightGBM only after release gate. |
| Metrics and release targets | PR-AUC >=1.25x prevalence AND paired improvement over strongest baseline; Brier <=0.95x baseline; report ROC-AUC, F1, recall at <=5 alerts/100 ward-days, ECE <=0.05. |
| Data feasibility gate | >=3 monsoons, >=100 positive ward-days across >=10 events, valid ward polygons and audited negatives. Otherwise publish rainfall exposure index, explicitly non-probabilistic. |
| Uncertainty / imbalance / explanations | Apply R/C/A shared protocol for the stated problem. Classes use training-only weights; regression has no class balancing. M09 presence-background outputs are relative scores; M10 clustered CI; M17 event-level bootstrap; M20 inherits base uncertainty, no new truth. |
| Failure modes | Under-reporting, changed drains, unmatched ward boundaries and urban rain cells; no house-level flood depth. |
| Retraining / monitoring | Shared cadence and drift contract above; source stale/label revision triggers quarantine. For M01 monitor target coverage, slice metrics and specific failure conditions above. Any changed target definition creates a new model version and untouched test. |
| Executable work | T089, T090, T091; configs/models/m01.yaml |

