# 17. Portfolio and interview pack

### README structure

| Order | Content |
|---|---|
| 1 | One-sentence problem, live demo link, current supported states and exact experimental/official disclaimer. |
| 2 | Screenshot and90-second walkthrough; show provenance, interval and accuracy page in image. |
| 3 | Architecture/data flow; core design choices; no-card cost ledger; current source health. |
| 4 | Reproduction commands, Python3.12 environment, data access/licences, minimal sample workflow and tests. |
| 5 | Model results table with actual test dates, rows/events, strongest baseline, metric difference/CI, status and model-card links. |
| 6 | Analysis findings; source/label limitations; failed experiments; privacy/security choices and operational runbooks. |
| 7 | Data/code licences, attribution, contribution instructions, roadmap and dated changelog. Never claim every listed stretch feature is shipped. |

### One case-study write-up per model

Use reports/models/mXX.md: problem and intended use -> data/label provenance and coverage -> target definition -> time/spatial split diagram -> baseline -> hypothesis and candidate -> leakage audit -> metrics with paired CI and calibrated uncertainty -> failure slices -> computational cost -> deployment decision -> monitoring/retraining -> reproducibility hashes -> what changed after review. Include one positive result and one negative lesson, with real numbers only.

### Three-minute demo script

| Time | Narration / evidence |
|---|---|
| 00:00-00:25 | â€œThis map combines public state data and shows what each source actually supports.â€ Open Karnataka and point to source freshness. |
| 00:25-00:55 | Click a market/place, show current facts, forecast interval and source. Explain scheduled versus live markers. |
| 00:55-01:25 | Open model accuracy. Compare baseline, test window and95%CI. Show a model that remains research-only. |
| 01:25-01:55 | Switch to Kerala2018 reconstruction and explain why it is not a record of this app operating then. |
| 01:55-02:20 | Show Delhi live/stale state if access exists, otherwise explain the truthful fallback and collection plan. |
| 02:20-02:45 | Ask a question, open its citation, then demonstrate quota or insufficient-evidence fallback. |
| 02:45-03:00 | Show pipeline/tests and cost ledger. State one measured improvement, one limitation and next experiment. |

### Resume bullets: replace placeholders only from frozen evidence

Data engineering: â€œBuilt a versioned pipeline integrating [N] licensed public datasets across3 Indian states, processing [N] records/day with [X]% valid coverage and point-in-time replay.â€

Data science: â€œEvaluated [N] forecasting/classification models using time and spatial holdouts; improved [metric] by [X]% over [baseline],95%CI [L,U], on [dates / independent events].â€

ML engineering: â€œDeployed daily batch forecasts with calibrated80% intervals, model/data lineage and rollback; measured [coverage]% coverage and [latency] inference time.â€

LLM/product: â€œImplemented multilingual cited retrieval with [N] reviewed evaluation cases, [X]% supported claims and quota-safe fallback on a Rs0 hosting budget.â€

Do not substitute target thresholds, prior-project0.33 PR-AUC, available data volume or proposed features for achieved results. A negative finding can be an interview strength when the experiment is reproducible.

### Interview talking points

| Question | Answer structure |
|---|---|
| Why one project for several roles? | Trace one observation from ingestion/schema checks through EDA, hypothesis, time-safe features, baseline comparison, forecast and map citation. |
| How did you prevent leakage? | Show available_at/issue_at join, purged horizon, training-only transforms, historical-vintage limits and a test that fails on future data. |
| Why not deep learning everywhere? | Show measured baseline and challenger results, sample size, compute budget and failure slices. |
| What is causal about rain-to-price? | Separate association, predictive precedence and causation; describe pre-trends, spillovers and confounders. |
| How does it stay free? | Show actual quota ledger, static predictions, finite archive policy, no billing-enabled fallback and graceful degradation. |
| What would you improve with more time? | Prioritize independent labels, forecast vintages, geographical coverage and language evaluation before new model families. |

# 18. Glossary of concepts

| Concept | Plain-English meaning |
|---|---|
| Data contract | A testable agreement about columns, types, units, meanings and valid values. |
| ETL / pipeline | Extract data, transform it into consistent form, then load/publish it. |
| Schema / schema drift | The structure of records; drift is an unexpected change in that structure. |
| Provenance / lineage | Where a value came from and which transformations/model versions produced it. |
| Point-in-time / as-of join | Join only data that was available when the prediction would have been issued. |
| Bitemporal data | Keep both the time a fact describes and the time the system learned it. |
| Forecast vintage | A particular forecast run, identified by its issue time and model version. |
| Hindcast | A retrospective prediction using historical data; may not reproduce the information actually available then. |
| Feature / target / label | A feature is an input; target is the quantity to predict; label is the observed target used for learning/evaluation. |
| Lag / rolling window | A past value or summary over a preceding time interval. |
| Leakage | Training or evaluating with information that would not have been available at prediction time. |
| Baseline / champion / challenger | A simple reference, currently approved model, and proposed replacement. |
| Persistence | Predict that the latest observed value will continue. |
| Seasonal naive / climatology | Use a matching earlier season/time, or a long-term seasonal average/distribution. |
| Train / tune / calibration / test | Fit parameters; choose settings; adjust probability/interval behaviour; finally assess untouched data. |
| Rolling-origin validation | Repeat evaluation by moving the forecast origin forward while training only on earlier data. |
| Spatial block / purging | Keep neighbouring areas or shared events together; remove rows whose target windows overlap split boundaries. |
| Bias / variance / overfitting | Systematic error; sensitivity to sample changes; learning noise that fails on new data. |
| Regularisation | Constraints or penalties that discourage an overly complex model. |
| Ridge / logistic regression | Linear models for numeric outcomes or class probabilities, often strong simple references. |
| Random forest | Many trees trained on varied samples, combined to reduce instability. |
| Gradient boosting / LightGBM | Trees added sequentially to correct remaining error; LightGBM is an efficient implementation. |
| Tweedie model | A distribution family useful for nonnegative skewed targets with possible zeros. |
| ARIMA / SARIMAX | Time-series models of lagged values/errors, with seasonal and external-variable extensions. |
| Hyperparameter / Optuna TPE | A modelling setting selected before fitting; TPE searches promising settings within a bounded budget. |
| Early stopping | Stop training when validation performance stops improving. |
| Class imbalance / prevalence | Rare positive cases compared with negatives; prevalence is the positive fraction. |
| Precision / recall / F1 | Fraction of alarms correct; fraction of true events detected; a harmonic combination of both. |
| PR-AUC / ROC-AUC | Area under precision-recall curve or true-positive/false-positive-rate curve; rare-event usefulness needs the first plus alert burden. |
| MAE / RMSE / MAPE | Mean absolute error; square-root mean squared error that emphasizes large misses; percentage error unreliable near zero. |
| Brier score / calibration / ECE | Squared probability error; agreement between predicted probability and observed frequency; a binned summary of calibration error. |
| Platt / isotonic calibration | Fit a logistic probability correction or a monotonic mapping using held-out calibration data. |
| Quantile / pinball loss | A distribution percentile; a loss designed to learn a chosen percentile. |
| Prediction interval / confidence interval | A range for an individual future outcome versus uncertainty in an estimated statistic. |
| Conformal prediction | Use held-out residuals to adjust intervals; time dependence and distribution shift can weaken its coverage guarantees. |
| CRPS | A score for a full predictive distribution; rewards sharp, well-calibrated distributions. |
| Bootstrap / paired block bootstrap | Resample observations to estimate uncertainty; blocks preserve dependence and pairing keeps baseline/model comparisons fair. |
| SHAP / permutation importance | Ways to describe input contributions to model outputs or the effect of scrambling an input; neither proves causation. |
| Drift / PSI | A change in input or outcome patterns; PSI compares binned distributions and is a heuristic warning, not a statistical proof. |
| Moran I / spatial weights | A measure of spatial similarity; weights define which areas count as neighbours. |
| Variogram / kriging | How differences grow with spatial distance; a model-based spatial interpolation method using that relationship. |
| H3 / CRS / EPSG | A hexagonal spatial index; a coordinate reference system; a registry code identifying one. |
| GeoParquet / PMTiles | Columnar files with geospatial metadata; a single-file archive of map tiles served by byte ranges. |
| SPI | Standardized precipitation index: rainfall deficit/excess compared with a fixed reference distribution. |
| NDVI / SAR | Red-near-infrared vegetation index; radar imagery that can observe through clouds but has other distortions. |
| CMIP6 / scenario / ensemble | A climate-model intercomparison collection; a conditional future pathway; multiple model runs or members. |
| Theil-Sen / Mann-Kendall | Robust median slope estimator; a trend test whose dependence assumptions need attention. |
| ADF / KPSS / stationarity | Tests related to whether series properties stay stable; their null hypotheses differ. |
| Fixed effects / event study | Control stable group differences; estimate changes around an event over pre/post windows. |
| Confounder / pre-trend / overlap / spillover | A common cause; existing change before exposure; comparable groups; effects crossing into controls. |
| Granger predictive precedence | Whether past values add predictive information after included lags; not proof of causality. |
| HAC / clustered standard errors | Uncertainty corrections for serial dependence or shared group shocks. |
| Multiple testing / Benjamini-Hochberg FDR | Many simultaneous tests increase false findings; BH controls expected false-discovery proportion under its assumptions. |
| MAD / robust z / IsolationForest | Median absolute deviation; scaled distance from a robust centre; anomaly detector based on isolating unusual points. |
| Tensor / batch / epoch | A multidimensional numeric array; examples processed together; one pass through training data. |
| Activation / ReLU / sigmoid / softplus | Nonlinear transformations; positive-part,0..1 probability-shaped, and smooth positive-valued functions. |
| CNN / U-Net / skip connection | Image filters; encoder-decoder segmentation network; connections that preserve fine-scale information. |
| IoU / Dice / segmentation | Intersection over union; overlap score related to F1; assigning a class to each pixel. |
| LSTM / GRU | Gated recurrent networks that maintain a learned state through a sequence; GRU is typically simpler. |
| Transformer / attention / head | A network that mixes token representations according to learned relevance; attention heads learn different relationships. |
| MuRIL / IndicBERT | Pretrained language encoders developed for Indian-language tasks; actual performance needs local evaluation. |
| TFT / N-BEATS / graph neural network | Neural forecasting families using temporal attention or basis blocks; networks that learn through connected nodes. |
| Embedding / cosine similarity | A vector representing text; angle-based measure of vector similarity. |
| RAG / chunk / BM25 / reciprocal-rank fusion | Retrieve evidence before generating; a document segment; lexical ranking; combine ranked lists using inverse rank. |
| Token / context / prompt injection | A model text unit; supplied input window; untrusted text attempting to override instructions. |
| Faithfulness / citation support | Whether a claim follows the evidence; whether its cited source actually backs it. |
| Cross entropy / BCE / loss | Learning objectives for class probabilities, binary probabilities, or any mismatch being optimized. |
| Optimizer / AdamW / learning-rate schedule | Parameter-update rule; adaptive optimizer with weight decay; how update size changes during training. |
| Dropout / weight decay / gradient clipping | Regularisation by dropping activations; penalizing large weights; limiting update-driving gradient magnitude. |
| Mixed precision / GPU memory | Compute with multiple numeric precisions to save resources; memory needed for weights, activations and optimizer state. |
| ONNX / safetensors | Portable model computation format; weights format avoiding executable pickle loading. |
| GTFS / GTFS-realtime / headway | Scheduled transit format; live transit extension; time gap between consecutive vehicles. |
| TLE / SGP4 | Two-line orbital elements; a standard propagation algorithm estimating satellite position. |
| PWA / service worker / Web Push | Installable web app; browser background/cache code; server-initiated notifications requiring subscription identifiers. |
| CSP / CORS / secret | Browser content restrictions; cross-origin access policy; credential that must never appear in public code. |
| WCAG / accessible name | Web accessibility guidelines; machine-readable control label used by assistive tools. |
| Idempotence / atomic publish / rollback | Repeating a job does not duplicate results; switch a complete release at once; return to an earlier valid release. |

# 19. Appendix A: full task list as YAML

This is the full task list again, generated from the exact same objects as section16. The PDF also embeds tasks.yaml as a file attachment for lossless extraction, avoiding page-header/footer artefacts from copy/paste. The one YAML block begins below and ends after the final task. Fields are id,title,phase,state,depends_on,files,steps,commands,acceptance,hours. Commands are for PowerShell in the repository root.

```
